Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for this Next.js project.

## Changes Made:

### 1. Installed Package
- Added `@vercel/analytics` package to dependencies
- Used npm to install the package (project uses npm as package manager)

### 2. Updated Files
**Modified: app/layout.tsx**
- Added import: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/next';`
- Added `<Analytics />` component inside the `<body>` tag in the root layout
- Followed the official Vercel documentation for Next.js App Router setup

**Modified: package.json**
- Added `@vercel/analytics` to dependencies

**Modified: package-lock.json**
- Updated lockfile to reflect the new dependency and its sub-dependencies

## Implementation Details:

The installation follows the official Vercel Web Analytics quickstart guide for Next.js App Router:
- Fetched latest documentation from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart
- Used the App Router approach (app/layout.tsx) as this is a Next.js 16 project
- Placed the Analytics component at the end of the body tag to avoid interfering with existing UI

## Verification:

✓ Build completed successfully with `npm run build`
✓ TypeScript compilation passed
✓ Smoke tests passed
✓ No linting errors introduced

## Next Steps:

To activate analytics:
1. Deploy the project to Vercel
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics section)
3. Analytics data will appear in the dashboard after deployment and user visits

The Analytics component is now properly integrated and will automatically start tracking page views and web vitals when deployed to Vercel.
